  "id": "Compound::DB00973 - Disease::DOID:1936",
  "verbalization": "Ezetimibe, an antilipidemic agent, is implicated in the pathogenesis of atherosclerosis through a multi-step mechanism. The evidence suggests that Ezetimibe causes Forgetfulness, which is then mediated by Rosuvastatin, a drug that treats atherosclerosis. Similarly, Ezetimibe causes Immune-mediated Necrotising Myopathy, which is also mediated by Rosuvastatin and linked to atherosclerosis. Both pathways share a common link to Rosuvastatin, which is an organonitrogen heterocyclic compound and an antilipidemic agent. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role for Ezetimibe in modulating lipid metabolism and immune responses, both of which contribute to atherosclerosis. The shared mechanism of action through Rosuvastatin underscores the importance of antilipidemic agents in the development of atherosclerosis, suggesting that Ezetimibe may have a broader impact on cardiovascular health through its interaction with lipid-lowering drugs.",
